<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottonian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/919">919</a>–1024</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Liudolfings, Liudolfing dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ottonian dynasty, also called the Liudolfings, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> family that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, from 919 to 1024. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> was the first king, and his son Otto I defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the Lechfeld in 955 and was crowned emperor in 962. Otto II, Otto III and Henry II followed. Their reign saw a flowering of art and learning known as the Ottonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family's earliest known member is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Liudolf (d. 866), who held large estates on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> river west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichsfeld">Eichsfeld</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. About 852 he and his wife Oda, with Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altfrid">Altfrid of Hildesheim</a>, founded Brunshausen Abbey, which after its move to Gandersheim became the family monastery and burial ground. In 869 his daughter Liutgard married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a>, son of the Carolingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>.</p><p>Liudolf's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-illustrious">Otto the Illustrious</a> was, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, already a candidate for the East Frankish crown in 911, when it passed instead to the Franconian duke Conrad I. Otto died in 912, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Elected king in May 919, Henry won the support of the Franconian, Bavarian, Swabian and Lotharingian dukes and in 933 defeated the Hungarians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-riade">Battle of Riade</a>. Otto I put down revolts by his brother Duke Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and his son Duke Liudolf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, reducing the dukes to royal subjects, and made the Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> a base of royal power. He was crowned emperor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a>, married his heir to Theophanu, niece of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a>, and in 968 established the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>.</p><p>Otto II was defeated by the Saracens at the Battle of Stilo in 982, faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-slav-rising">Great Slav Rising</a> in 983 and died that year aged 28, leaving a three-year-old son for whom Empress Theophanu acted as regent. Otto III installed his confidants Bruno of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Gerbert of Aurillac</a> as popes and in 1000 travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-gniezno">Congress of Gniezno</a>; expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1001, he died the next year aged 21. Henry II, of the Bavarian line, was crowned king on 7 June 1002 and emperor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-viii">Pope Benedict VIII</a> on 14 February 1014, and founded the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in 1007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Henry II's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunigunde-of-luxembourg">Cunigunde of Luxembourg</a> was childless, and with his death in 1024 the dynasty became extinct. The crown passed to Conrad II of the Salian dynasty, a great-grandson of Liutgarde, daughter of Otto I; the Salians held the imperial throne until 1125. Pope Eugene III canonized Henry II in 1146. Historians disagree about how the Ottonians governed: those following Karl Leyser stress oral and ritual means, while others, such as David Bachrach, argue for the continued use of writing in administration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottonian dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians debate when the <b>Liudolfings</b> became dukes in a formal sense; their power grew from the defence of East Francia against Vikings, Slavs and Magyars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Saxony, ruled by the <b>Liudolfing</b> family, produced the kings of East Francia from 919, beginning with Henry the Fowler, and his son Otto I was crowned emperor in 962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded the <b>Ottonian dynasty</b>, and his son Otto I became emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal grants from the <b>Ottonians</b> onwards gave the archbishops temporal lordship over Mainz and scattered lands along the Main, the Rhine, in Hesse and the Eichsfeld.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-mainz">Electorate of Mainz</a></li><li><blockquote>It received royal protection and immunity, and under the <b>Ottonians</b> it became a favoured house of the ruling dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li><li><blockquote>As the family monastery of the <b>Liudolfings</b>, who became the Ottonian kings, it enjoyed royal favour, and in 919 it was made a royal abbey.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-gandersheim">Imperial Abbey of Gandersheim</a></li></ul></section>
